    "body": "Unnatural Products has risen from founder Joshua Schwochert’s garage to an acquisition deal, valued at up to $1.15 billion, to join argenx.\nArgenx is putting up to $1.15 billion on the line to buy out peptide partner Unnatural Products, a company that also counts Novartis and Merck as collaborators.\nThe deal includes $550 million upfront plus undisclosed contingent value rights that will be offered upon certain milestones and bring the total potential deal value to $1.15 billion, according to a Monday press release .\nArgenx, which already had a minority stake in Unnatural Products (UNP) and has worked with the biotech for two years, will combine its immunology antibody engineering capabilities with UNP’s macrocyclic peptide discovery engine to develop oral or long-acting therapies for serious immune-mediated diseases.\n“After working together for more than two years, we also know there is a strong fit in how we work: science first, entrepreneurial and focused on patients,” argenx CEO Karen Massey said in a statement. “We want to preserve what has made UNP successful while bringing the strengths of both teams together.”\nUNP’s team will continue to operate from Santa Cruz, California, which will become argenx’s peptide discovery hub. The existing company will continue to lead discovery work within argenx. The parent company will provide clinical development and commercialization expertise to advance the resulting medicines.\nThe drug discovery biotech rose from humble beginnings. UNP was founded in 2016, originally operating out of Chief Scientific Officer Joshua Schwochert’s garage. Led by Cameron Pye as CEO, the pair believed the industry was overlooking the therapeutic potential of macrocyclic peptides.\nLarge biopharma companies seemed to agree, with UNP establishing four major partnerships in less than five years. Earlier this year, Novartis offered $100 million upfront and up to $1.7 billion in milestones to develop new medicines for undruggable targets in cardiovascular disease. In 2024, Merck shelled out $220 million to work with UNP in oncology, and BridgeBio also has a long-term partnership with the biotech.\nUNP raised $45 million in a series B financing in March, with Merck’s venture capital arm and argenx participating.\nArgenx and UNP announced their initial partnership in July 2025 . While the upfront fee was not disclosed, the licensing deal included $1.5 billion in future milestones.",
